// **Every one of these thirteen was silently accepted until F212**, with its whole output thrown
// away. `PURE_BUILTINS` is the list of names whose discarded result is an error, and the Map/Set
// pairing of F195-F201 renamed fourteen of its entries -- `set_union` to `union`, `map_keys` to
// `keys`, `set_insert_all` to `add` -- each rename leaving the old name in the list and adding
// none of the new ones. The stale entries cost nothing, a call to a name that does not resolve
// failing earlier; the **missing** ones were the bug, and `tools/check_builtins.py` now holds
// every entry to being a name that can reach the gate, so half a rename cannot pass again.
//
// Each line is the pure immutable member of a set whose builder member writes in place, so
// discarding it cannot have been meant. The builder members stay discardable, which is
// `discard_overload_purity_allowed`'s half. `add` and `remove` needed `#[effectful]` on their
// builder members first: those answer a bool *and* write the container, so listing the name
// without the attribute would have refused "make sure x is in here".
fn main() {
    val m = Map<string, int> { "a": 1 }
    val m2 = Map<string, int> { "b": 2 }
    val s = Set<int> { 1 }
    val s2 = Set<int> { 1, 2 }
    union(s, s2)
    intersect(s, s2)
    difference(s, s2)
    is_subset(s, s2)
    to_array(s)
    keys(m)
    values(m)
    merge(m, m2)
    get(m, "a")
    get_or(m, "a", 0)
    filter(s, fn(x: int) -> bool { return x > 0 })
    add(s, 9)
    remove(m, "a")
}
